# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# TLS for the node, and for every handle it issues.
#
# ⭐ **On-demand TLS is not an optimization here, it is what makes a handle namespace
# work.** A handle on this network is a domain name, so a server issuing handles has to
# answer on a hostname per account — and it cannot know them in advance, because they are
# created by people signing up. Enumerating them into a certificate list would mean
# reissuing on every signup. Instead Caddy asks the server whether a name it has just been
# asked for is one of ours, and gets a certificate on the spot if it is.
#
# 🚨 **`ask` is a gate rather than a formality, and removing it turns this into an open
# certificate mill.** Without it, anybody who points a DNS record at this machine makes it
# request a certificate on their behalf, which is rate-limited by the certificate authority
# and will eventually take the real names down with it.
#
# ⚠️ **The gate is answered by this Caddyfile's own loopback site, not by the server**, and
# the block below says why: the image some nodes run has no `/tls-check`, and a gate pointed
# at a route that is not there refuses every name.
{
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://127.0.0.1:2020/tls-check
	}
}

# ─── The on-demand gate ───
#
# ⭐ **On-demand TLS asks one question before issuing anything: is this name ours?** The answer
# has to come from the server's own account table, or any name pointed at this box gets a
# certificate in our account's name and the certificate authority's weekly limit becomes the
# lever somebody uses to break renewals for real handles.
#
# 🚨 **The gate is answered here, not by the server, because the image this node runs has no
# `/tls-check`.** `ghcr.io/bluesky-social/atproto:pds-spaces-alpha` carries no such route (the
# reference distribution's does), so a gate pointed straight at it refuses every name and the
# whole namespace loses TLS on the next certificate-cache miss. That is not hypothetical: it
# took `anthers.social` and every handle down for ten minutes on 2026-10-27, when a restart
# cleared a cache that had been hiding the missing route since the alpha cutover.
#
# ⚠️ **The check is still the server's, through a route it does answer.** A handle this server
# hosts resolves at `/.well-known/atproto-did`, so the gate asks that with the queried name as
# the Host and passes the answer through: 200 for a handle the account table holds, 404 for
# anything else. The apex is allowed by name, because `anthers.social` is the service's own
# address rather than a handle.
http://127.0.0.1:2020 {
	@apex {
		path /tls-check
		query domain={$PDS_HOSTNAME}
	}
	handle @apex {
		respond "" 200
	}
	@ask {
		path /tls-check
	}
	handle @ask {
		rewrite * /.well-known/atproto-did
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000 {
			header_up Host {query.domain}
		}
	}
	handle {
		respond "Not a certificate request this node answers." 403
	}
}

# The apex answers the protocol; everything under it is somebody's handle.
*.{$PDS_HOSTNAME}, {$PDS_HOSTNAME} {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}

	# ─── The front door ───
	#
	# ⭐ **The server's own root is ASCII art and a paragraph, and it is the first thing a
	# stranger meets.** Every account Anthers issues lives on this host, every hosted sign-in
	# is sent here to authenticate, and the reset mail lands on its account page, so the root
	# is the one page nobody arrived looking for. This serves a real one at exactly `/`.
	#
	# 🚨 **One exact path, and that is the whole scope.** `path /` matches the root and nothing
	# else, so OAuth, `/xrpc/*`, `/account`, the TLS gate and every handle's own answers keep
	# going to the server untouched. A matcher that caught a prefix, or a `handle_path` that
	# rewrote a path, would take the identity server down rather than dress it up, and an
	# identity server that answers the wrong page is worse than one that answers a plain one.
	#
	# ⚠️ **Static, and served from this container rather than the server's.** The page has no
	# build step and no script, because the host whose job is to answer when the hub does not
	# is the last place to add a second thing that can be down.
	#
	# ⭐ **A node with no page laid down keeps the server's own**, rather than 404ing. This
	# bundle also runs on a stranger's machine, where a front door reading "every account
	# Anthers issues lives here" would be false and a hard-coded link to anthers.org/signup
	# would be somebody else's advert. Dropping a file at /pds/landing/index.html turns the
	# front door on; leaving that directory empty leaves the node exactly as it was.
	@frontDoor {
		host {$PDS_HOSTNAME}
		path /
	}
	handle @frontDoor {
		encode zstd gzip
		root * /pds/landing
		@hasPage file /index.html
		handle @hasPage {
			file_server
			header {
				# The page changes when somebody edits it, not between requests. Ten
				# minutes keeps an edit from needing a purge and still answers a reader
				# instantly.
				Cache-Control "public, max-age=600"
				-Server
			}
		}
		handle {
			reverse_proxy http://localhost:3000
		}
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy http://localhost:3000
	}
}

# The public status page — served from this droplet on purpose, a failure domain apart
# from the hub it reports on. The heartbeat's cron regenerates /pds/status/index.html
# every minute (scripts/status-page.sh), so this site serves a static file and answers
# whether or not the hub does: the reader checking whether anthers.org is down gets an
# answer even during the outage the question is about.
#
# ⚠️ **The name is not a handle and must not look like one.** It lives outside the
# wildcard's namespace (`status.anthers.org` does not end in `.anthers.social`), so the
# on-demand gate below cannot be tricked into minting it — it is named here explicitly,
# which is the Caddy form of "we meant this one".
#
# 🚨 **The DNS record for this name must exist and point here** (see the Runbook's § on
# this page): a Caddyfile site for a name no DNS record carries mints nothing.
#
# ⚠️ **No `tls` directive at all, rather than an empty `tls {}` block** — the empty block
# is invalid Caddyfile syntax ("wrong argument count or unexpected line ending after '}'",
# learned by crash-looping the live Caddy for twenty seconds on 2026-10-08), and it is not
# needed: a site address spelling `https://` gets automatic HTTPS with a normally-issued
# certificate, and this name is named explicitly here (not under the wildcard's
# `on_demand`), so it provisions at startup like any ordinary first-site cert.
https://status.anthers.org {
	encode zstd gzip
	root * /pds/status
	file_server
	header / {
		# The page answers per minute, never worth caching at an intermediary.
		Cache-Control "no-cache"
		-Server
	}
}
